{"id":"ruger-gp100","slug":"ruger-gp100","name":"GP100","aliases":["Ruger GP-100","GP100 revolver","GP-100 revolver","Ruger GP100 revolver","Ruger GP-100 revolver","Ruger GP100"],"kind":"firearm","category":"revolvers","subcategory":"Revolver","description":"The GP100 is a family/line of double action five- (.44 Special), six- (.357 Magnum, .38 Special, & 10mm Auto), seven- (.357 Magnum and .327 Federal Magnum), or ten-shot (.22 long rifle) revolvers made by Sturm, Ruger & Co., manufactured in the United States.","history":"The GP100 was an evolution of an earlier Ruger double-action revolver, the Security Six.\nIt was introduced in 1985 as a second generation of the Ruger double-action, exposed-hammer revolvers intended to replace Ruger's Security-/Service-/Speed-Six line.\nIt was made stronger with the intent to fire an unlimited number of full-power .357 Magnum rounds. The first significant change was introduced with the Ruger Redhawk and that involved a new triple-locking cylinder mechanism with a lever on the crane rather than using the end of the ejector rod to lock.\nAnother change was the use of stronger steels and the redesigned, beefier shape of the frame.\nTraditional revolver frames had exposed metal at the front and rear of the grips, with the frame determining the shape of the grips. This meant that to have a round butt concealed carry version and a square butt holster or target version of the same gun usually meant having two different frame shapes.\nThe GP100 series, instead, used a small rectangular \"peg\" grip large enough to enclose the hammer spring and strut. The grips could then be any shape desired, as long as they were large enough to enclose the peg.\nThis was not an innovation, having been used by Dan Wesson and some High Standard revolvers that predated the GP100.\nThe Dan Wesson patent (U.S. patent 3,683,535) was granted in 1972 and Ruger's patent (U.S. patent 4,625,445) was granted in 1986.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge","value":"•10mm Auto"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":"•.22 long rifle"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":"•.327 Federal Magnum"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":"•.357 Magnum"},{"label":"Action","value":"Double-action revolver"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"5-shot cylinder (.44 Special)"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"6-shot cylinder (.357 Magnum, .38 Special, & 10mm Auto)"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"7-shot cylinder (.357 Magnum & .327 Federal Magnum)"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"10-shot cylinder (.22 Long Rifle)"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Weight","value":"35–45 oz"},{"label":"Length","value":"8-11.5 in"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"2.5 in","numeric":2.5,"unit":"in"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"3 in","numeric":3,"unit":"in"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"4 in","numeric":4,"unit":"in"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"4.2 in","numeric":4.2,"unit":"in"}]},{"group":"Performance","items":[{"label":"Effective Range","value":"55–110 yd"},{"label":"Maximum Range","value":"+/− 550 yd"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"Sturm, Ruger & Co."},{"label":"Produced","value":"1985–present"},{"label":"Number Built","value":"734,500"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"United States"}]}],"specIndex":{"Cartridge":"•10mm Auto","Action":"Double-action revolver","Feed System":"5-shot cylinder (.44 Special)","Weight":"35–45 oz","Length":"8-11.5 in","Barrel Length":"2.5 in","Effective Range":"55–110 yd","Maximum Range":"+/− 550 yd","Manufacturer":"Sturm, Ruger & Co.","Produced":"1985–present","Number Built":"734,500","Origin":"United States"},"images":{"hero":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/Ruger_GP100_.357_Stainless.jpg","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruger_GP100_.357_Stainless.jpg","width":2048,"height":1365,"license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","a